## A loop around the loop

Some tasks need several tools in a row, where each step depends on the last. Take this one: "Find
which nursery rhyme mentions a cat, and count its words." The model has to search, read the result,
then count. An <GlossaryPopover term="agent">agent</GlossaryPopover> handles this with one more loop:
the harness calls the model, runs the tool it asks for, adds the result, and calls it again, until
the model answers without asking for a tool. Step through a real run:

<AgentStepper />

<At levels="simple">

So there are two loops. Inside, the model writes each reply one token at a time, as in <ChapterRef to="generating-text" />.
Around that, the harness keeps calling the model, running tools, and adding results until the model
is done.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

There are two loops here. Inside each model call is the token loop from <ChapterRef to="generating-text" />: predict, pick,
append. Around it is the harness loop: call the model, run a tool, add the result, repeat. This
pattern of interleaving a model's reasoning with actions was popularized by the ReAct
paper.<Cite id="yao2023" />

</At>

The model never saw the rhyme until the search returned it, and it never counted anything: it
copied the rhyme into a call to the word counter, then reported the counter's answer, 30 words.

## Who decides the next step

<At levels="simple">

An agent is one end of a range. Along it, what changes is who picks the next step: code written in
advance, or the model.

- **A fixed recipe.** Code runs the same steps every time, and the model only writes the text.
- **A fork.** Code sends each question down one of a few set paths, as <ChapterRef to="routing" />
  passes a question to a bigger model when the small one isn't sure.
- **One tool.** The model decides once whether to use a tool, as in <ChapterRef to="tools" />.
- **A loop.** The model picks every next step and when to stop, as in the run above.

The further along, the more kinds of task it can take on, but the more it costs and the more one
mistake can lead to another. A good rule is to use the simplest one that does the job.<Cite id="anthropic-agents" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

An agent is one end of a range, and what changes along it is who decides the next step: code
written in advance, or the model.

- **A fixed chain.** Code runs set steps in order, such as summarize and then translate. The model
  only writes text.
- **A router.** Each request goes down one of a few fixed paths, as <ChapterRef to="routing" />
  passes a question on to a bigger model when the small one seems unsure.
- **One tool call.** The model decides whether to call a tool, and with what, once, as in <ChapterRef to="tools" />.
- **A loop.** The model decides what to do after every result, and how many steps to take, as in
  the run above.

Anthropic calls systems whose path is fixed in code **workflows**, and keeps "agent" for systems
where the model directs its own steps.<Cite id="anthropic-agents" /> A loop suits tasks whose steps
can't be known in advance, but it costs more, takes longer, and lets one wrong step lead to the
next, so their advice is to use the simplest setup that works.<Cite id="anthropic-agents" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

Their list of workflows also includes running several calls at once and combining their answers,
and having one call check another's work and send it back for changes.<Cite id="anthropic-agents" />

</At>

## The harness makes the rules

<At levels="simple">

This run worked partly because of rules in the harness, not the model:

- It lets the model ask for only one tool at a time.
- Its instructions tell the model to use a tool for counting instead of guessing.
- It stops after 8 model calls, so a confused agent can't go on forever.

Each call also re-reads the whole conversation so far, which grows with every step.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

This run worked partly because of choices in the harness, not the model:

- **One tool at a time.** The harness stops the model right after its first tool call, so it can't
  write a second call before seeing the first result.
- **Instructions.** The system prompt (<ChapterRef to="the-model-is-not-the-product" />) told it to use a tool for counting instead of
  guessing.
- **A step limit.** The harness allows at most 8 model calls, so a confused agent can't loop
  forever. Each call also re-reads the whole growing conversation: here 301, then 394, then 483
  tokens.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### What the loop costs

Because every call re-reads the whole conversation, the work grows faster than the number of
steps. If the first prompt is $p_0$ tokens and each step adds about $a$ more, call $k$ (counting
from 0) reads $p_k = p_0 + k\,a$ tokens, and $K$ calls read

$$
\sum_{k=0}^{K-1} p_k = K\,p_0 + a\,\frac{K(K-1)}{2}
$$

tokens in all, a total that grows with the square of the number of steps. This run read
301 + 394 + 483 = 1,178 tokens to give one short answer, and each step added about 90.

Real harnesses lean on the fact that each prompt starts exactly like the last one: prompt caching
(<ChapterRef to="caching" />) lets the provider reuse its work on the unchanged start instead of reading it again.<Cite id="openai-prompt-caching" /><Cite id="anthropic-prompt-caching" />
When the prompt outgrows its budget, the harness drops, summarizes, or retrieves, as in <ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" />.

</At>

## Tools as code

<At levels="simple">

In the run above, the model asked for one tool, waited for the answer, then asked for the next.
Another way is to let it write a short program that uses the tools, like this made-up one:

```python
rhyme = search_rhymes("cat")[0]
print(count_words(rhyme["text"]))
```

The harness runs the program and gives the model what it printed, so one reply does both steps.
Agents that write programs like this finish more tasks than ones that ask for one tool at a
time.<Cite id="wang2024codeact" /> But now the harness runs code the model wrote, so it has to run it
somewhere safe, cut off from anything that matters.<Cite id="smolagents-secure" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The run above wrote one tool call per model call: search, wait for the result, then count. Another
design lets the model write its actions as a short program in which every tool is a function, like
this made-up one:

```python
rhyme = search_rhymes("cat")[0]
print(count_words(rhyme["text"]))
```

The harness runs the program and adds whatever it printed to the prompt. One model call now does
what took two, and a program can loop over results, keep them in variables, and combine tools in
ways that one call at a time can't. Across 17 models, writing actions as Python code succeeded up
to 20% more often than writing them as JSON or text in a fixed format.<Cite id="wang2024codeact" />

The catch is that the harness now runs code the model wrote. A plain mistake, or instructions
planted in a tool result (<ChapterRef to="prompt-injection" />), becomes code that runs. So code
agents run it in a sandbox: an interpreter that allows only listed imports and stops runaway
loops, or a separate container or machine with nothing worth harming.<Cite id="smolagents-secure" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

When a program fails, the error message comes back as its result, so the model can read it and fix
the code on its next turn.<Cite id="wang2024codeact" />

</At>

<RecapCard>

- An agent is a model called in a loop by a harness that runs its tool calls and feeds the results
  back in.
- Workflows fix the steps in code; an agent lets the model decide them. Use the simplest that
  works.
- The loop stops when the model answers without a tool call, or when it hits a step limit.
- Some agents write their actions as a short program, so one call can do several steps; that code
  has to run in a sandbox.
- Each step re-sends the whole conversation, so the prompt keeps growing.

<At levels="deep">

- $K$ calls read about $K\,p_0 + a\,K(K-1)/2$ tokens in all: the cost grows with the square of the
  steps.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** every tool result is text the harness pastes into the prompt: search results, web
pages, files. What if some of that text gives the model instructions of its own?
